\textbf{Subject:} Manuscript Transfer and Submission --- ``Signal Hierarchy Theory for Biological Petri Nets: Formal Semantics of Hierarchical Regulatory Control''

\vspace{12pt}

Dear Editor,

I am pleased to continue submission of our manuscript entitled \textbf{``Signal Hierarchy Theory for Biological Petri Nets: Formal Semantics of Hierarchical Regulatory Control''} for consideration as a Research Article in PLOS ONE, following its transfer from PLOS Computational Biology. We believe PLOS ONE provides an ideal venue given its emphasis on scientific soundness, methodological rigor, and broad interdisciplinary readership.

\section*{Manuscript Summary}

Following transfer from PLOS Computational Biology, we submit our manuscript addressing a fundamental unsolved problem: **predicting cellular commitment thresholds from first principles**. When \textit{Bacillus subtilis} enters irreversible sporulation, what ATP concentration triggers commitment? Experimental measurements show 2.21 $\pm$ 0.18 mM, yet no computational method can predict this \textit{a priori} without parameter fitting. This limitation reflects a deeper gap: no formalism unifies metabolic dynamics with regulatory control while preserving quantitative predictive capability.

We present **signal hierarchical Petri nets** enabling first-principles threshold prediction from network topology and independently-measured biochemical parameters. Applied to \textit{B. subtilis}, the formalism predicts ATP commitment threshold at 2.38 mM—within 7\% of experimental measurements—using only literature-reported parameters (BRENDA database, biochemistry textbooks, systems biology publications). **Zero parameters were fitted to the commitment threshold.** This breakthrough is impossible with existing formalisms (FBA lacks regulation, Boolean networks treat metabolites as parameters, classical Bio-PN cannot express signal consumption).

The formalism extends Bio-PN from 5-tuple to 13-tuple adding signal hierarchy: signal places $\Psi$ (information channels), signal flow arcs $F_s$ (consumption semantics), layer function $\lambda$ (hierarchical assignment), thresholds $\theta$ (enablement conditions). Two proven theorems guarantee correctness: signal flow graphs must be acyclic, and lower-layer depletion disables higher-layer transitions.

\section*{Impact and Applications}

This breakthrough enables practical applications across multiple domains:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Synthetic Biology}: Design metabolic decision circuits with formal commitment guarantees, predicting threshold reliability before expensive laboratory construction. Example: optimizing glucose-sensing bistable switches for biofuel production.

\item \textbf{Drug Discovery}: Identify ATP-dependent therapeutic targets by quantifying which control points are most sensitive to metabolic perturbations. Example: predicting cancer cell commitment to apoptosis under glycolysis inhibition.

\item \textbf{Systems Biology}: Unify metabolism and regulation in single executable models, eliminating ad-hoc interfaces between separate modeling frameworks. Example: genome-scale metabolic models incorporating transcriptional regulation.

\item \textbf{Precision Medicine}: Predict individual cellular responses to dietary or pharmacological metabolic interventions based on patient-specific enzyme expression profiles.
\end{itemize}

The formalism addresses a recognized gap explicitly stated in recent systems biology reviews: existing frameworks separate metabolism from regulation, coupled only through informal parameter dependencies. Our unified approach treats energy status (ATP, NADH, GTP) simultaneously as metabolite and regulatory signal, enabling bidirectional quantitative analysis impossible with current methods.

\section*{Why PLOS ONE?}

Following transfer from PLOS Computational Biology, we believe PLOS ONE provides a better fit for this work due to its evaluation criteria and readership:

\textbf{1. Scientific Soundness Over Impact Speculation:} Our work provides rigorous mathematical foundations (formal definitions, proven theorems, quantitative validation achieving 7\% threshold prediction accuracy) with comprehensive validation on one well-characterized biological system (\textit{Bacillus subtilis}). PLOS ONE's emphasis on methodological rigor and reproducibility aligns perfectly with our contribution---we demonstrate **breakthrough capability** (first-principles threshold prediction) rather than claiming immediate broad applicability requiring validation across dozens of organisms. The method is sound; broader impact requires community adoption over time.

\textbf{2. Interdisciplinary Breadth:} PLOS ONE's diverse readership spanning experimental biology, computational science, and bioengineering ensures this work reaches scientists who can exploit it: experimentalists seeking threshold predictions for their systems, synthetic biologists designing decision circuits, and metabolic engineers optimizing production strains. PLOS Computational Biology's specialist audience would miss key application communities.

\textbf{3. Reproducibility and Open Science:} All code (Python implementation with MIT license), models (SBML format), and validation data are publicly available at \url{https://github.com/simao-eugenio/shypn}. Complete parameter provenance tables document all 47 parameters with literature sources, enabling independent verification. This exemplifies PLOS ONE's commitment to transparent, reproducible science.

\textbf{4. Methodological Innovation:} While the formalism introduces theoretical components (13-tuple extension), the primary contribution is **methodological**---a new way to solve previously unsolvable biological problems. PLOS ONE recognizes methodological advances that enable new questions rather than requiring high-impact biological discoveries from first publication.

\textbf{5. Honest Scope Assessment:} We openly acknowledge current limitations: validation demonstrates capability on one comprehensive biological system rather than surveying dozens of organisms. This honest framing---establishing predictive capability before claiming universal applicability---aligns with PLOS ONE's scientific integrity standards, whereas high-impact journals often expect broader immediate claims.

\section*{Relation to Published Work and Target Audience}

\textbf{How This Work Provides Worthwhile Addition to Literature:}

Existing frameworks exhibit fundamental limitations this work overcomes:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Classical Biological Petri Nets} (Heiner et al., 2008; Koch et al., 2011): Test arcs model non-consumptive catalysis but cannot express signal depletion creating irreversibility. Our signal flow arcs enable commitment analysis impossible in classical Bio-PN.

\item \textbf{Hybrid Petri Nets} (David \& Alla, 1992; Liu et al., 2013): Mix continuous/discrete dynamics but treat metabolism and regulation as separate models connected through software interfaces. Our 13-tuple provides single unified formalism spanning both domains.

\item \textbf{Flux Balance Analysis} (BiGG Models, Orth et al., 2010): Optimizes metabolic fluxes assuming all enzymes exist at sufficient concentrations, ignoring transcriptional control. Cannot predict thresholds or model commitment.

\item \textbf{Boolean/ODE Gene Regulatory Networks} (Thomas, 1973; de Jong et al., 2003): Model regulation but treat metabolites as external parameters. Cannot analyze how ATP depletion affects transcription factor activation.

\item \textbf{Chemical Reaction Network Theory} (Feinberg, 1987): Addresses steady states under mass-action kinetics but lacks regulatory arcs and hierarchical semantics. Cannot distinguish commitment from equilibration.
\end{itemize}

Our formalism uniquely combines: (1) quantitative metabolic dynamics (continuous kinetics), (2) regulatory control (hierarchical signal flow), (3) commitment semantics (consumptive arcs creating irreversibility), and (4) first-principles threshold prediction (no fitting to commitment behavior). No existing framework provides all four capabilities simultaneously.

\textbf{Target Scientist Communities:}

This work will interest multiple communities:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Systems Biologists}: Seeking unified metabolism-regulation modeling frameworks eliminating manual interface construction between separate models

\item \textbf{Synthetic Biologists}: Designing metabolic decision circuits requiring quantitative threshold prediction for robust circuit construction

\item \textbf{Metabolic Engineers}: Optimizing production pathways where regulatory feedback limits flux---our formalism quantifies regulation-metabolism coupling

\item \textbf{Computational Biologists}: Developing Petri net tools and formalisms---our 13-tuple extension provides roadmap for implementation

\item \textbf{Mathematical Biologists}: Studying cellular decision-making, bistability, and commitment---our basin boundary formalization provides analytical tools

\item \textbf{Experimental Biologists}: Measuring commitment thresholds (sporulation, differentiation, apoptosis)---our predictions provide testable hypotheses
\end{itemize}

The formalism's impact spans theoretical computer science (Petri net extensions), applied mathematics (dynamical systems), and experimental biology (threshold measurements), ensuring broad relevance across PLOS ONE's interdisciplinary readership.

\section*{Closing Remarks}

The manuscript emphasizes **biological problem-solving capability** rather than theoretical novelty alone. While the 13-tuple formalism introduces mathematical components (signal places, signal flow arcs, layer functions), the primary contribution is **solving the previously unsolvable problem** of first-principles threshold prediction. We frame validation as comprehensive demonstration: \textit{Bacillus subtilis} sporulation provides ideal test case exhibiting energy-gated developmental commitment with well-characterized experimental measurements, enabling rigorous quantitative validation achieving 7\% prediction accuracy.

This honest scope assessment---establishing predictive capability on one comprehensive system before claiming broad applicability---reflects scientific integrity and contrasts with premature generalization. The formalism's theoretical soundness (proven theorems, formal operational semantics) combined with quantitative validation demonstrates capability; broader adoption across diverse organisms requires community engagement over time.

We acknowledge the transfer from PLOS Computational Biology and believe PLOS ONE's evaluation criteria (scientific soundness, methodological rigor, reproducibility) better match our contribution's nature: a breakthrough enabling new quantitative analyses rather than high-impact biological discoveries requiring this methodology's first publication.

Thank you for considering our manuscript. All code, models, and validation data are publicly available at \url{https://github.com/simao-eugenio/shypn} (MIT license). I look forward to your response and am happy to provide any additional information required.

